// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE BOTTOM STRIP AND WHAT STACKS ON TOP OF IT
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Three rules in this file used to spell the same geometry as three unrelated
// literals, and one of them was wrong: `#active-notifications` and
// `.debug-notification` both sat at `bottom: 38px` while the bar they stack
// above measures 40px. So the lowest toast was drawn two pixels UNDER the
// status bar and clipped by it — reported from the live site as "the one at
// the bottom is partially obscured by the status bar".
//
// 38 was not a wrong guess about a fixed number; it was a number that stopped
// tracking the thing it was derived from. `#status-base` is `2em` tall at
// `font-size: 1rem` with `0.25em` of padding on each side and no
// `box-sizing: border-box`, so its outer height is 2rem + 0.5rem = 2.5rem =
// 40px. Nothing linked the two, so the bar could grow and the toasts would
// keep addressing where it used to end.
//
// So the bar's height is a value, and the stack's offset is COMPUTED from it
// plus the very gap the toasts already put between each other. The clearance
// between the lowest toast and the bar is now the same measurement as the
// clearance between two toasts, by construction rather than by coincidence —
// `ci/test/noir_edit_persists_probe.mjs` measures the two and
// `ci/test/noir-edit-persists.sh` asserts they are equal, so a future change
// to `NOTIFICATION_STACK_GAP` moves both and keeps the gate green, while a
// change to only one of them cannot be spelled.

NOTIFICATION_STACK_GAP = 0.5rem
  // The gap between stacked toasts, and — via NOTIFICATION_STACK_BOTTOM —
  // the gap between the lowest toast and the status bar.

STATUS_BAR_CONTENT_HEIGHT = 2rem
STATUS_BAR_VERTICAL_PADDING = 0.25rem
  // Breathing room so the tab curve pseudo-elements are visible above the
  // tabs; mirrored by the side strip's padding-left/right.

STATUS_BAR_OUTER_HEIGHT = STATUS_BAR_CONTENT_HEIGHT + 2 * STATUS_BAR_VERTICAL_PADDING
  // 2.5rem. `#status-base` is content-box, so the paddings are outside the
  // declared height and a reader of `bottom:` has to add them back.

NOTIFICATION_STACK_BOTTOM = STATUS_BAR_OUTER_HEIGHT + NOTIFICATION_STACK_GAP
  // 3rem. Clears the bar, then leaves exactly one inter-toast gap above it.

// THE BUILD PANEL'S TEXT COLOUR — REPORTED AS *"hardly readable due to low
// contrast"*, AND THE WORST OF IT WAS NEVER CHOSEN AT ALL.
//
// This is the same defect as the status bar two commits ago, in the same file:
// `body` (`components/legacy.styl`) declares a `background-color` and NO
// `color`; `.build-panel` declared a `background-color` and no `color`; `#build`
// declared a `background-color` and no `color`; and NO ancestor in between
// declares one either — `#auto-hide-overlay`, `#auto-hide-overlay-body`,
// `#auto-hide-overlay-content`, `#root-container` and `#dom-root` are all
// background-only. BUILD is a STANDALONE auto-hide pane (`ui/layout.nim`
// registers it as one, `auto_hide.doShowOverlayImpl` reparents it into
// `#auto-hide-overlay-content`), so it is NOT inside `.lm_goldenlayout` and
// does not pick up that sheet's `color: colors-ui-text-primary-label`. The
// declaration simply ran out.
//
// So two tiers inherited the USER-AGENT default. Measured in Chromium against
// the compiled theme, on this panel's own `colors-ui-surface-base-panel`
// (grey-700, `#282828`):
//
//   tier                                     web      Electron
//   `.build-output-line` (located, no sev)   1.42:1   1.05:1
//   `.build-command-label` (idle/running)    1.42:1   1.05:1
//
// The web host serves no Bootstrap, so there the inherited value is
// `canvastext` → BLACK. `src/frontend/index.html` loads
// `third_party/bootstrap-4.3.1-dist/css/bootstrap.css` AFTER the theme link and
// Bootstrap declares `body { color: #212529 }`, so in the desktop shell the
// same text is `#212529` — 1.05:1, which is not "low contrast", it is INVISIBLE.
// Both are worse than the 1.22:1 the status bar shipped.
//
// The rest of the panel was chosen, but chosen as raw hex against no measured
// surface, and the error tier misses AA:
//
//   `.build-stderr` / `.build-line-error` / `.build-header.build-failed`
//     `#f85149`                                            4.40:1  (AA is 4.5)
//   `.build-stop-btn`  `rgba(248,81,73,0.7)`               2.80:1
//   `.build-stop-btn.disabled`  (that, times `opacity:0.3`) 1.30:1
//
// EVERY COLOUR BELOW NOW RESOLVES THROUGH A TOKEN, because the numbers above
// are only true of `#282828` and a raw hex cannot follow the surface it was
// picked against. The ramp for the control buttons is three DISTINCT tokens
// rather than one token times `opacity`, so the disabled state is a colour
// change — an `opacity` multiplier is what drove the stop button to 1.30:1, and
// it is the same mistake `tests/gui/tests/debug-controls/toolbar-marks-contrast.spec.ts`
// already refuses for the debugger strip.
//
// `-primary` and not `-hover` for the severity tiers: `-primary` is the design
// system's BASE state and `-hover` is its hover state, and the base state is
// what compiler output is read in. It costs nothing here — every `-primary`
// severity token clears AA on this surface by a wide margin.
//
// The measured result, identical in both themes (see the note on Block B below
// for why "both themes" is one number here):
//
//   tier                            before    after   token
//   `.build-output-line`            1.42:1   13.29:1  colors-ui-text-primary-body
//   `.build-command-label`          1.42:1    8.81:1  colors-ui-text-primary-label
//   `.build-stderr`                 4.40:1    7.77:1  colors-ui-text-error-primary
//   `.build-line-error`             4.40:1    7.77:1  colors-ui-text-error-primary
//   `.build-header.build-failed`    4.40:1    7.77:1  colors-ui-text-error-primary
//   `.build-line-warning`           5.84:1    8.74:1  colors-ui-text-warning-primary
//   `.build-header.build-succeeded` 5.80:1   12.17:1  colors-ui-text-success-primary
//   `.build-ctrl-btn.active`        5.80:1   12.17:1  colors-ui-text-success-primary
//   `.build-ctrl-btn`               4.83:1    8.81:1  colors-ui-text-primary-label
//   `.build-ctrl-btn.disabled`      1.30:1    4.68:1  colors-ui-text-primary-label-subtle
//   `.build-duration`               6.27:1    7.11:1  colors-ui-text-primary-caption
//   `.build-stop-btn`               2.80:1    7.77:1  colors-ui-icon-error-primary
//   `.build-stdout` / `.build-line-info`  13.29:1 (unchanged)
//
// The two translucent hover SCRIMS stay as they are — they are surfaces, not
// ink, and the ink measured against what they composite to still clears AA:
// `rgba(255,255,255,0.1)` over `#282828` is `#3e3e3e` and carries
// `colors-ui-text-primary-body` at 9.64:1; `rgba(248,81,73,0.15)` is `#472e2d`
// and carries `colors-ui-icon-error-on-color` at 8.56:1.
//
// `src/tests/gui/tests/build/build-panel-contrast-guard.spec.ts` measures all
// of it and fails on anything under 4.5:1, and one of its arms re-paints the
// shipped `#f85149`/inherited-black and asserts the guard rejects them — a
// threshold nothing can violate is not a gate.
.build-panel
  height: 100%
  display: flex
  flex-direction: column
  background-color: colors-ui-surface-base-panel
  // The declaration whose absence is the bug. Everything in this subtree that
  // does not name a colour now inherits a CHOSEN one instead of `canvastext`.
  //
  // The comment goes ABOVE the property and is indented to the SAME column as
  // it: an indented comment placed after a declaration opens a child block in
  // Stylus, which silently closed `.build-panel` here and re-emitted every
  // nested rule below at the top level — `.build-header`, `.build-ctrl-btn`,
  // `.build-duration` and `.build-output-container` compiled as GLOBAL
  // selectors one specificity step weaker than written. It measured the same
  // in a fixture that contains nothing else and would not have in the app.
  color: colors-ui-text-primary-body

  .build-header
    padding: 4px 8px
    font-size: 13px
    flex-shrink: 0
    display: flex
    align-items: center
    justify-content: space-between
    color: colors-ui-text-primary-label

    &.build-failed
      color: colors-ui-text-error-primary

    &.build-succeeded
      color: colors-ui-text-success-primary

  .build-header-controls
    display: flex
    align-items: center
    gap: 4px
    flex-shrink: 0

  .build-ctrl-btn
    width: 22px
    height: 22px
    display: flex
    align-items: center
    justify-content: center
    border-radius: 3px
    cursor: pointer
    font-size: 12px
    color: colors-ui-text-primary-label
    user-select: none

    &:hover
      color: colors-ui-text-primary-body
      background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1)

    &.disabled
      color: colors-ui-text-primary-label-subtle
      cursor: default
      pointer-events: none

    &.active
      color: colors-ui-text-success-primary

  .build-stop-btn
    color: colors-ui-icon-error-primary

    &:hover
      color: colors-ui-icon-error-on-color
      background-color: rgba(248, 81, 73, 0.15)

  .build-duration
    font-size: 12px
    color: colors-ui-text-primary-caption
    margin-left: 4px
    white-space: nowrap

  // THE BUILD PANEL'S FONT — `"FiraCode"` WAS A FAMILY NOBODY DECLARES.
  //
  // Reported as *"the font used in the build panel is not right"*, with no
  // further detail. Measured from the compiled theme (byte-identical to the
  // one noirstudio.dev serves): the whole design system declares exactly four
  // `@font-face` families — `SpaceGrotesk`, `SpaceMono`, `FiraMono` and
  // `FontAwesome`. **`FiraCode` is not one of them, and no FiraCode file
  // exists in the tree.** It is dead text in 21 places, four of them here.
  //
  // Elsewhere that mistake is survivable because the rule writes a fallback —
  // `components/shell.styl` and `components/problems.styl` say
  // `"FiraCode", monospace`, so the pane degrades to the platform's monospace
  // and merely looks off-brand. These four rules had NO second entry. An
  // unresolvable family with no generic fallback drops to the browser's
  // *default standard font*, which on Chrome is a PROPORTIONAL SERIF — so
  // column-aligned compiler output was being set in Times beside a terminal
  // set in SpaceMono. That is the visible difference the report is pointing
  // at, and the `!important` meant nothing downstream could rescue it.
  //
  // `SpaceMono` rather than `FiraMono` — which is declared, and is probably
  // what the typo was reaching for. The neighbours decide it: TERMINAL OUTPUT
  // is SpaceMono (`components/terminal.styl`), the EVENT LOG is SpaceMono,
  // and the editor is `CODE_FONT_FAMILY = "SpaceMono, monospace"`
  // (`frontend/utils.nim`). Build output is read against those, not against a
  // font the product uses nowhere.
  //
  // The generic fallback is written even though `SpaceMono` IS declared,
  // because that is the property whose absence turned a typo into a serif.
  .build-output-container
    flex: 1
    overflow: auto
    font-family: "SpaceMono", monospace !important
    font-size: 14px
    line-height: 24px
    white-space: pre-wrap

    .build-stdout, .build-stderr, .build-output-line
      min-height: 24px
      padding-left: 0.5em
      font-family: "SpaceMono", monospace !important
      font-size: 14px
      line-height: 24px
      white-space: pre-wrap

// The legacy Karax build panel. Same four declarations, same fix: the IsoNim
// panel above did not invent the typo, it copied it down from here.
//
// AND THIS BLOCK IS NOT ONLY LEGACY — IT IS WHAT PAINTS THE LIVE PANEL'S TEXT.
// `isonim_build_view.nim` gives the output container `id = "build"` (both the
// Web and the Mock renderer do), so every selector here is an ID selector
// matching inside `.build-panel`, and it OUTRANKS the class-only rules above:
// `#build .build-stdout` is (0,1,1,0) against `.build-panel
// .build-output-container .build-stdout` at (0,0,3,0). A colour written above
// and not here does not reach an output line. That is why the severity tiers
// live down here and why the base `color` has to be repeated.
//
// `#build-errors` is a separate pane that is NOT inside `.build-panel`, so it
// does not inherit the base colour declared there; without the `color` on this
// block it would keep inheriting `canvastext`.
//
// ONE PALETTE, BOTH THEMES — so the "check the other theme" question has a
// short answer here and it is not "it's fine". `colors-*` are Stylus
// compile-time variables from `styles/generated/`, and neither
// `default_white_theme.styl` nor `default_dark_theme.styl` redefines a single
// one of them (`grep '^colors-' on both returns nothing`). Compiling
// `default_dark_theme_electron.styl` and `default_white_theme_electron.styl`
// and diffing the two `.build-panel`/`#build` blocks gives ZERO differing
// bytes. The panel is `#282828` with the same ink in the light theme as in the
// dark one, so every ratio in the table above is one number, not two — and a
// future Light-mode token set will move both blocks together because both
// blocks name tokens.
#build, #build-errors
  background-color: colors-ui-surface-base-panel
  color: colors-ui-text-primary-body
  font-family: "SpaceMono", monospace !important
  font-size: 14px
  line-height: 24px
  white-space: pre-wrap

  .build-stdout, .build-error
    color: colors-ui-text-primary-body

  .build-stdout, .build-stderr, .build-error, .build-output-line
    min-height: 24px
    padding-left: 0.5em
    font-family: "SpaceMono", monospace !important
    font-size: 14px
    line-height: 24px
    white-space: pre-wrap

  .build-stderr
    color: colors-ui-text-error-primary
    font-style: italic

  .build-location
    cursor: pointer

  .build-clickable
    cursor: pointer
    &:hover
      text-decoration: underline

  // A located line whose severity parsed as `blsNone` — `lineClass` in
  // `isonim_build_view.nim` emits `build-output-line build-clickable` with NO
  // severity class for it, and it was in the geometry rule below and in no
  // colour rule anywhere. This is the 1.42:1 / 1.05:1 row.
  .build-output-line
    color: colors-ui-text-primary-body

  .build-line-error
    color: colors-ui-text-error-primary

  .build-line-warning
    color: colors-ui-text-warning-primary

  .build-line-info
    color: colors-ui-text-primary-body

  .build-error
    border: colors-ui-text-primary-body solid 1px

  overflow: auto

  height: 100%

#errors
  background-color: colors-ui-surface-base-panel
  color: colors-ui-text-primary-body

#active-notifications
  width: fit-content
  height: fit-content
  bottom: NOTIFICATION_STACK_BOTTOM
  display: flex
  // `column-reverse`, so the newest toast is at the BOTTOM of the stack and
  // the lowest one on screen is the FIRST DOM child. The clipped notification
  // in the report was therefore `:first-child`, not `:last-child`.
  flex-direction: column-reverse
  gap: NOTIFICATION_STACK_GAP
  position: fixed
  z-index: 100
  // HORIZONTALLY CENTRED, and this replaces `right: 8px`.
  //
  // THE DEFECT, MEASURED rather than described. On the live deployment at
  // revision b6e28026, with the durability notice raised and nothing else on
  // screen: the toast's rect is `[1126, 779, 466, 173]`, the CONSTRAINTS
  // pane's is `[1200, 66, 396, 902]`, and the two intersect over
  // **67,816 px²** — the pane's full 392px width for 173px of its height.
  // `elementFromPoint` at the centre of that intersection answers
  // `DIV.notification-message`, so the toast is not merely adjacent to the
  // pane's rows, it is painted over them.
  //
  // This is the one notice in the product that is raised on a first visit and
  // stays until it is dismissed, so the overlap is not a flash: it is the
  // default first screen, in every screenshot taken of it.
  //
  // WHAT THE SAME MEASUREMENT SAYS IS *NOT* WRONG, because the report also
  // described the text as clipped on its left edge (`…ort project`,
  // `…ected yet`). Asked of the element rather than of an image, on the
  // deployed site: `scrollWidth === clientWidth` for both the toast and its
  // `.notification-message`. The box is intact and the whole sentence is laid
  // out. The clipping was an artefact of cropping a screenshot to the
  // neighbouring PANE element, which cuts the toast at the pane's left border.
  // There is no horizontal overflow here to fix, and
  // `ci/test/noir_build_probe.mjs` now measures the property so the answer
  // does not have to be rediscovered.
  //
  // NOT CAUSED BY THE PREVIOUS TWO FIXES, which is worth stating because all
  // three touch this notice. 44abd587a moved this stack from `bottom: 38px` to
  // `NOTIFICATION_STACK_BOTTOM` — the vertical axis, and nothing else.
  // a0b9f8fb1 stopped the notice appearing three times, by fixing a component
  // re-subscription in `configureMiddleware`; no CSS at all. `right: 8px` has
  // been the anchor since the toasts were first placed (adc508550, March
  // 2025), and the notice only started landing on a pane when 3850bd9b8 moved
  // it out of its own body-level banner into this stack — that commit fixed a
  // notice which covered the STATUS BAR by putting it where it covers a PANE.
  //
  // WHY CENTRE RATHER THAN THE OTHER CORNER. In this product's three-column
  // edit layout the bottom corners belong to the two NARROW columns — FILES at
  // 20% and the right-hand column at 25% — and both are lists whose content
  // runs the full height of the pane, so a toast in either corner covers rows
  // somebody is reading. The centre belongs to the editor, which is 55% wide
  // and whose text is anchored to the TOP: the region a 466px toast occupies
  // there is empty in the common case. Moving it to `left: 8px` would also
  // collide with `.debug-notification`, which is already anchored there.
  //
  // WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO, stated because the next change to this area will
  // want it. Centring removes the measured overlap with the right-hand column;
  // it does not make a fixed-position toast structurally incapable of covering
  // content, and a listing that opens as an EDITOR tab in a split view would
  // sit under exactly this box. The durable fix is for the stack to occupy
  // layout rather than float over it — `#root-container`'s
  // `height: calc(100vh - …)` already reserves the status bar's height, and
  // reserving the stack's would guarantee zero overlap in every layout. That
  // relayouts GoldenLayout on every toast, so it is a design decision and not
  // a CSS one, and it is not smuggled in here.
  //
  // `left: 50%` + `translateX(-50%)` rather than `margin: auto`, because the
  // element is `position: fixed` with `width: fit-content` and no `right`, so
  // there is no resolved free space for `auto` margins to divide.
  //
  // The transform is safe against `status-footer-contract.ts`'s off-screen
  // guard: every one of its `FOOTER_REQUIRED_REGIONS` is inside `#status-base`
  // and this element is a sibling of that bar, not a child of it.
  left: 50%
  transform: translateX(-50%)


#status
  position: relative
  width: 100vw
  display: flex
  flex-direction: column-reverse
  font-family: "SpaceGrotesk"

  // Toasts stack above the bar and carry buttons; they must never become a
  // window handle if one ever overlaps the footer.
  > #active-notifications
    -webkit-app-region: no-drag


  #status-base
    // Hosts the auto-hide bottom tab strip (#auto-hide-bottom-strip) alongside
    // the status bar's own content, per
    // `codetracer-specs/Planned-Features/Auto-Hide-Panes.md` §3.1:
    //
    //   |-- Status Bar / Footer
    //   |     |-- Left: auto-hide tab labels for bottom-pinned panels
    //   |     |-- Right: existing status bar content (language, encoding, location)
    //
    // ... and §10.3, which places the collapsed-strip icon zone
    // (`#auto-hide-collapsed-icon-zone`) in the same bar.
    //
    // DO NOT hide `#status-base`'s children as a group, IN ANY SPELLING —
    // including ones that leave the box intact and only move it out of view.
    // A `> *:not(#auto-hide-bottom-strip) { display: none !important }` rule has
    // been written here twice — b27da3947 "feat: Redesign of the status bar"
    // and, after it was removed, 51a3e820e "fix: UI regressions" — and both
    // times it made the bar tabs-only.  A third attempt, 00fd68b7f "fix: status
    // bar styling", reached the same result with `position: absolute;
    // left: -9999px` on `#file-info-status` and `.status-right`, and shipped
    // undetected for two weeks because that was precisely the family the guards
    // below documented themselves as unable to see — they asked whether a
    // region has a box, and an off-screen region has one.  They now ask whether
    // the box is also where the user is looking, and the rule above is one of
    // their negative controls.  The wish behind all three is
    // reasonable: a quieter footer.  No such form can deliver it, because
    // every child of this bar is either required by the design or invisible
    // in production
    // already (enumerated on a live launch: `#auto-hide-collapsed-icon-zone`,
    // `#file-info-status`, `#auto-hide-bottom-strip`, `.test-movement` — which
    // renders only under `data.startOptions.inTest` — and `.status-right`).
    // There is no "legacy" residue left to hide; hiding the group means hiding
    // the design.  A quieter footer has to come from restyling or from removing
    // a specific readout in the design doc first.
    //
    // Concretely, the rule silently deletes three surfaces that no other part
    // of the UI provides:
    //
    //   * `.location-path` + `#copy-path-image` — the only place the current
    //     debug location (`path:line#ticks`) is displayed at all; nothing else
    //     in the frontend renders `StatusBaseModel.locationText`, and the only
    //     other copy-a-path affordance is the editor tab's "Copy full path"
    //     context-menu entry (`ui/layout.nim`), which copies the tab label,
    //     not the location the debugger is actually stopped at.
    //   * `#file-info-status` — language / encoding / `stable: ready`.
    //   * `#auto-hide-collapsed-icon-zone` — the `!important` beat
    //     `.collapsed-icon-zone.has-icons`, so the Auto-Hide-Panes §10.3 icon
    //     zone could never appear even when panels were collapsed.
    //
    // The layout the spec asks for falls out of the DOM order in
    // `viewmodel/views/isonim_status_view.nim` once nothing is force-hidden:
    // icon zone and `#file-info-status` on the left, the tab strip taking the
    // slack (`#auto-hide-bottom-strip { flex: 1 }`), `.status-right` (location
    // + copy button) pinned to the right edge.
    //
    // The constraint is checked against the BUILT stylesheet, on effect rather
    // than on wording, by
    // `src/tests/gui/tests/status-bar/footer-visibility-css-guard.spec.ts`:
    // it lays out this bar in a browser with the compiled theme CSS applied and
    // fails if any required region computes to no box, OR computes to a box
    // that lies entirely outside the viewport.  Any spelling that removes the
    // box trips it, however the selector is written — `:not()`, `:where()`,
    // `:has()`, an enumerated list, `@media`, `display`, `visibility`,
    // `opacity`, `transform: scale(0)`, or the same applied to an ancestor —
    // and so does any spelling that exiles it: `position: absolute/fixed` with
    // a large negative `left`/`top`/`right`, or `transform: translateX()`.
    // `status-bar-footer-contract.spec.ts` pins the same contract against the
    // real Electron app.
    //
    // What those two do NOT catch, so you know what you are not covered
    // against: rules that keep the box AT ITS PROPER COORDINATES and only
    // change the painting — `clip-path: inset(100%)`, the legacy
    // `clip: rect(0,0,0,0)` idiom, `filter: opacity(0)` or `filter: blur()`,
    // an opacity just above zero, or covering the bar with an overlay.  The
    // list is not prose only: it is `SURVIVING_HIDE_SPELLINGS` in
    // `src/tests/gui/page-objects/status-footer-contract.ts`, and the guard
    // runs it, so it cannot quietly go stale the way the previous one did.
    // Run the app and look at the footer before shipping anything in that
    // family.
    //
    // ONE MEMBER OF THAT FAMILY IS NOW COVERED, by a third guard rather than
    // by a stronger predicate in the first two.  Drawing the text in (or near)
    // the background colour used to pass everything, and it was not
    // hypothetical: this bar shipped its readouts in the user-agent default
    // BLACK on `#1b1b1b` — 1.22:1 — and the only reason nobody reported it
    // sooner is that `00fd68b7f` had them off-screen at the time.
    // `src/tests/gui/tests/status-bar/footer-contrast-guard.spec.ts` measures
    // what every region of this bar is painted against what is behind it, so
    // colour-only invisibility now fails a check.  The two colour entries in
    // `SURVIVING_HIDE_SPELLINGS` carry `caughtBy: "contrast"` and that guard
    // runs them, so the hand-off between the two files is asserted rather
    // than described.  Clipping, blurring and overlays remain uncovered.
    //
    // NOTE the guard reads the COMPILED theme, so it only sees an edit to this
    // file after `just build-once`.
    bottom: 0
    background-color: colors-ui-surface-primary-default
    // THE BAR DECLARES A TEXT COLOUR, because it declares a background.
    //
    // Reported from noirstudio.dev as *"the colors in the status bar are
    // difficult to read"*, and measured in the running product rather than
    // guessed: `body` (components/legacy.styl) sets `background-color` and no
    // `color`, this bar set `background-color` and no `color`, and no
    // ancestor in between sets one either — so every readout here inherited
    // the USER-AGENT default, which is `canvastext`.  With no `color-scheme`
    // declared anywhere that resolves to BLACK.  Read off the live page with
    // `getComputedStyle`: `#status-base` is `rgb(0, 0, 0)` on
    // `rgb(27, 27, 27)` — **1.22:1**, against a 4.5:1 requirement for normal
    // text.  `.file-info-status-language` ("Noir"),
    // `.file-info-status-encoding` ("UTF-8"), `#location-status`,
    // `.location-path` and `.build-identity` all inherited it.  That is the
    // complaint: not a colour chosen badly, a colour never chosen at all.
    //
    // It could not have been reported before now.  `00fd68b7f` had these
    // readouts parked at `left: -9999px` from 2026-08-18 until `74879f32c`
    // brought them back, so the black-on-near-black they had always been
    // painted in was off-screen the whole time and nobody could see it.
    //
    // WHY THIS TOKEN.  `colors-ui-text-primary-label-subtle` (grey-300,
    // `#919191`) is the QUIETEST token in the ramp that clears 4.5:1 on this
    // surface — 5.47:1.  The next one down, grey-350 `#818181`, measures
    // 4.42:1 and misses.  That matters here beyond arithmetic: this bar has
    // been made tabs-only three times by people who wanted a quieter footer
    // (see the note below), so raising its contrast is in tension with a real
    // and recurring wish, and the answer is the dimmest legible value rather
    // than the brightest available one.  It stays visibly below the attended
    // level the bar already uses for an active tab
    // (`colors-ui-text-primary-label`, grey-150, 10.29:1 here), so the
    // readouts read as ambient rather than as something demanding attention.
    //
    // Declared on the bar rather than on each readout because the defect is a
    // MISSING INHERITED VALUE, and its fix belongs at the root of the
    // inheritance it was missing from — one declaration covers every current
    // child and every child added later.  Descendants that own a colour
    // (`#operation-status`, `.disconnected-status`, `#copy-path-image`, the
    // strip tabs) are unaffected; all four were measured above threshold and
    // are deliberately left alone.
    //
    // `src/tests/gui/tests/status-bar/footer-contrast-guard.spec.ts` measures
    // this, and every other painted colour in the bar, against the compiled
    // theme in a browser.
    color: colors-ui-text-primary-label-subtle
    // THE BAR'S HEIGHT IS READ FROM A VALUE, and `#active-notifications`
    // reads the same one. These used to be `2em`/`0.25em` here and a bare
    // `38px` there, which is how the toast stack came to address a bar edge
    // that had moved. See the header of this file.
    height: STATUS_BAR_CONTENT_HEIGHT
    font-size: 1rem    // reset from 0.75rem so the rem values above land 1:1
    position: fixed
    width: 100%
    z-index: 100 !important
    display: flex
    align-items: stretch
    overflow: visible   // allow tab curve pseudo-elements to bleed above the bar
    padding-top: STATUS_BAR_VERTICAL_PADDING    // curves visible above the tabs
    padding-bottom: STATUS_BAR_VERTICAL_PADDING // mirrors side-strip padding

    // The footer drags the window, like the caption bar does.
    //
    // Same arrangement as `#menu` (components/menu_bar.styl) and
    // `.session-tab-bar` (components/session_tabs.styl): the bar drags, and the
    // things it holds opt out, so only the space between them is a handle.
    // Declared on the bar rather than on the empty area because there is no
    // element for the empty area — it is whatever the tab strip does not fill.
    -webkit-app-region: drag

    // Readouts and controls behave normally: `.status-right` carries the
    // location text, and text inside a drag region cannot be selected.
    > *
      -webkit-app-region: no-drag

    // ...except the tab strip, which is `flex: 1` and so spans the whole bar
    // (components/auto_hide.styl).  Marking it `no-drag` with the rest would
    // freeze the footer end to end, because the empty area IS the strip's
    // unfilled remainder — the same mistake the caption bar's left region made,
    // where a `no-drag` container froze the 300-odd px of bar beside the debug
    // toolbar.  So the strip drags and its tabs opt out individually.
    > #auto-hide-bottom-strip
      -webkit-app-region: drag

      .auto-hide-strip-tab
        -webkit-app-region: no-drag

